// 74_apophis.cpp -- the asteroid 99942 Apophis for T & L > "Solar system" (74_solarsys.cpp).
//
// Apophis crosses the Earth's orbit and meets the Earth every few years; on 2029-04-13 21:46 TDB it
// passes 38 011 km from the Earth's centre, and that pass turns it from an Aten (a = 0.92 AU) into an
// Apollo (a = 1.10 AU). So the straight-line mean elements the planets and the dwarf planets run on
// (74_ephem.cpp) cannot hold it: fitted to Horizons over 1900-2029 they were 25 deg off.
//
// Instead: JPL Horizons' heliocentric OSCULATING elements (solution JPL#220, 2024-06-25; ELEMENTS,
// CENTER 500@10, J2000 ecliptic) at epochs picked so that, between two consecutive rows, the blend of
// the two-body orbits run from each is within 1e-4 AU of Horizons -- checked at every day of
// 1900-2100 and every 10 minutes of 2029-04-06..21 (75 195 samples; worst 0.997e-4 AU, 0.008 deg).
// 1e-4 AU is the class of the app's own Earth (JPL Table 2a). Base rows every 512 days, more where the
// Earth pulls (the 2029 pass alone takes rows minutes apart). Outside 1900-2100 there is no position.
//
// Columns: epoch (JD, TDB), a (AU), e, I, Omega, omega, M (deg).
